---
import Essay from '../../layouts/Essay.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import LoopVideo from '../../components/LoopVideo.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';
import structure from '../../assets/work/piezo-motor-structure.jpg';
import setup from '../../assets/work/piezo-setup.png';
import loop from '../../assets/work/piezo-control-loop.png';
import position from '../../assets/work/piezo-position-comparison.png';
import switching from '../../assets/work/piezo-gen-comparison.png';

/* Project page; see work/test2fail.astro for how these pages are built.
   This one is my master's thesis: figures and results come from the oral
   defence slides (July 2026) and the repository's README and benchmarks. */
const repo = `${SITE.github}/piezo-motor-visual-servo`;
---

<Essay
  docNo="05"
  title="Visual servo control of a piezoelectric motor"
  description="Flora Liu's master's thesis: vision-based closed-loop control of a 9 × 6 mm piezoelectric ultrasonic motor, with a 120 FPS camera, a Keysight function generator driven over SCPI, and PID heading control."
  thesis="A camera watches a 9 × 6 mm motor, and a PID loop steers it."
  next={{
    href: '/work/outdoor-weathering',
    kicker: 'Next project',
    title: 'Outdoor weathering monitor',
    promise: 'A year of one-minute weather readings, made answerable in MySQL and Excel.',
  }}
>
  <div class="sub">
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p class="opener">
        A motor the size of a fingernail drifts off course. How do you steer it without adding a
        single sensor to it?
      </p>
      <p>
        My master's thesis. The motor is a 9 × 6 mm plate that walks on traveling waves. Left
        alone it drifts, and any sensor mounted on it would add weight. So a camera watches it
        instead, and a PID loop corrects it 120 times a second.
      </p>
      <div class="proj-videos">
        <LoopVideo
          src="/media/work/piezo-demo-straight.mp4"
          alt="Tracking view of a straight run: the motor is boxed in red on a grid, with live position, angle, speed and frame rate overlaid."
        />
        <LoopVideo
          src="/media/work/piezo-demo-rotation.mp4"
          alt="Tracking view of a rotation run, with the same live overlay."
        />
      </div>
      <p class="aside">
        <a href={repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/floraliu-dev/piezo-motor-visual-servo ↗</a>
        · Python · OpenCV · SCPI · Presented at SPIE Smart Structures 2026
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <h2>The motor</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        A PZT sheet on a stainless steel plate, both 9 × 6 mm, free on every edge. Exciting two
        vibration modes together, out of phase, turns standing waves into a traveling wave that
        pushes the plate along. One pair of modes moves it in a straight line; another pair turns it
        on the spot. The electrode layout decides how strongly each mode is driven.
      </p>
      <Figure
        no="1"
        wide
        src={structure}
        alt="Exploded view of the motor: a 9 by 6 mm stainless steel plate under a PZT sheet with split electrodes. Electrodes A1 and A2 are marked; Y linear motion uses modes Φ20 and Φ21, Z rotation uses Φ21 and Φ31."
        caption="Motor structure and electrode layout. Linear motion uses modes Φ20 + Φ21; rotation uses Φ21 + Φ31."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <h2>Closing the loop with a camera</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        A camera adds no weight and touches nothing, and it sees both position and heading at once.
        A global-shutter camera above the motor feeds a computer; the computer sets the function
        generator, whose signal a power amplifier raises to drive the motor.
      </p>
      <Figure
        no="2"
        src={setup}
        alt="Setup sketch: a computer connected to a camera module and a function generator; the function generator feeds a power amplifier wired to the motor on a grid."
        caption="Experimental setup."
      />
      <p>
        Each frame, the software finds the motor, filters its position and heading, compares them
        with the target, and turns the error into a drive command. Capture, image processing and
        control run on separate threads so they overlap.
      </p>
      <Figure
        no="3"
        wide
        src={loop}
        alt="Block diagram: reference minus measured output gives the error; mode decision and PID give a command, sent through a compound SCPI write to the function generator and HV amplifier, which drive the actuator. A camera with Kalman and EMA filtering measures the motion at 120 fps and feeds it back."
        caption="The control loop. Camera, filtering and control run as three threads at 120 FPS."
      />
      <dl class="proj-stats">
        <div><dt>Feedback rate</dt><dd class="num">120.6 FPS</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Camera to command</dt><dd class="num">12.4 ms</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Dropped frames</dt><dd class="num">0 of 2,500</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Calibration error</dt><dd class="num">0.094 mm</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <h2>Does the control work?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Driving straight, the uncontrolled motor wanders off line. With heading control it holds its
        course, and it is faster too: top speed rises from 67.7 to 122.4 mm/s.
      </p>
      <Figure
        no="4"
        src={position}
        alt="Two trajectories from the same start. Without control the path wanders left, ending 10.2° off; with control it stays nearly straight, 2.6° off."
        caption="One straight run with and without heading control: 10.2° of drift down to 2.6°."
      />
      <p>
        Turning on the spot to a target angle between 3° and 8°, it settles within about 1.2° of
        the target.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <h2>Switching drive modes in under a millisecond</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <p>
        Changing direction first meant re-uploading the waveform: about 268 ms, some 30 frames with
        no new command. Preloading the waveforms and sending every change as one compound SCPI
        command brought it under a millisecond, more than 600 times faster.
      </p>
      <Figure
        no="5"
        src={switching}
        alt="Log-scale bar chart: a full waveform upload takes 268 ms; compound commands take 0.3 to 0.4 ms, 670 to 850 times faster."
        caption="Mode-switch latency, full upload versus compound SCPI commands (log scale)."
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sub">
    <div class="sub__head">
      <h2>Try it</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="sub__body">
      <ul>
        <li>
          Run from source: <code>pip install -r requirements.txt</code>, then <code>python main.py</code>.
          Camera, mode and instrument address are set in <code>config.py</code>.
        </li>
        <li>Every run saves a tracked video, a raw video, a CSV and plots.</li>
        <li>
          <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/benchmarks/benchmark_summary_report.txt`} target="_blank" rel="noopener"
            >Full latency and throughput benchmarks ↗</a
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</Essay>

<style>
  .proj-videos {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 0.75rem;
    max-width: 30rem;
    margin: 1.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .proj-videos :global(video) {
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 6px;
  }

  .proj-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(9rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1rem 1.5rem;
    margin: 1.75rem 0 0;
  }

  .proj-stats dt {
    font-size: var(--fs-meta);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .proj-stats dd {
    margin: 0.2rem 0 0;
    font-size: var(--fs-lede);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
